issue.body:
I'm still learning React, so it's possible I'm misunderstanding something.

I'm trying to use React with Immutable.js, and hit a stumbling block, by upgrading to React 0.13. There a [wiki page](https://github.com/facebook/immutable-js/wiki/Immutable-as-React-state) on the immutable.js repository, that explains how to use immutable as a react state, and it provides the following example (I transpiled it to ES3):

``` js
'use strict';

var React = require('react');
var Immutable = require('immutable');

var Component = React.createClass({

  getInitialState: function() {
    return Immutable.Map({count:0});
  },

  handleClick: function() {
    this.replaceState(this.state.update('count', function(v) { return v + 1 }));
  },

  render: function() {
    return (
      <div onClick={this.handleClick}>
        {this.state.get('count')}
      </div>
    );
  }

});

React.render(<Component />, document.body);
```

This works great in 0.12, but in 0.13, you will receive the following error when clicking on the div:

```
TypeError: this.state.get is not a function
this.state.get('count')
```

Putting a breakpoint on the error, and inspecting this.state with a debugger reveals that all the methods on this.state have disappeared.

Is this the expected behavior in 0.13?

